<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The window behind the app, when the system is in dark mode.

  Both themes are dark, and both name the app's own colour rather than the
  system's.

  `flutter create` gave the light one `Theme.Light.NoTitleBar` and
  `?android:colorBackground`, which is white. The app never reads the system
  setting — brightness is the farmer's own toggle, remembered, defaulting to
  dark — so on a phone whose system is light the window was white before the
  first frame and white again behind the UI on every resize and rotation, a
  flash of the wrong colour on a screen that is #0B0F0C.

  This matches the **default**, which is what a launch is until somebody
  chooses otherwise; `make splash-check` reads that default out of `app.dart`
  and fails if it moves. A farmer who has chosen light gets one dark frame,
  which is the price of a window painted before any code has run.

  The two files stay because Android picks by the system setting and would fall
  back to the light one; they say the same thing so that it does not matter
  which it picks.
-->
<resources>
    <!-- While the process starts. -->
    <style name="LaunchTheme" parent="@android:style/Theme.Black.NoTitleBar">
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@drawable/launch_background</item>
    </style>
    <!-- Once it has, and behind the Flutter UI for the life of the activity. -->
    <style name="NormalTheme" parent="@android:style/Theme.Black.NoTitleBar">
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@color/launch_ground</item>
    </style>
</resources>
